<section class="phero"><div class="phero-media"><img class="bg" src="/assets/img/photos/calculator-sticky-note.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/calculator-sticky-note-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/calculator-sticky-note.webp 960w" sizes="100vw" width="960" height="631" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" data-parallax="0.1"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><i aria-hidden="true">›</i><span aria-current="page">Claim terms</span></nav><p class="kicker">Reference</p><h1>Insurance Claim Terms Glossary</h1><p class="lede">39 words you’ll meet in policies, estimates and appraisal awards, defined in plain English. Type in the box to filter.</p></div></section>
<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose"><div class="filter"><label for="gloss-filter">Search the glossary</label><input id="gloss-filter" type="search" placeholder="Try “depreciation” or “umpire”" autocomplete="off"><p class="hint" data-gloss-count></p></div><div class="az"><a href="#g-A">A</a><a href="#g-C">C</a><a href="#g-D">D</a><a href="#g-E">E</a><a href="#g-F">F</a><a href="#g-I">I</a><a href="#g-L">L</a><a href="#g-M">M</a><a href="#g-O">O</a><a href="#g-P">P</a><a href="#g-R">R</a><a href="#g-S">S</a><a href="#g-T">T</a><a href="#g-U">U</a><a href="#g-X">X</a></div><dl class="glossary"><div class="gterm"><dt id="g-A">Actual cash value (ACV)</dt><dd>Replacement cost minus depreciation. Many policies pay ACV first and the rest after repairs.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Additional living expense (ALE)</dt><dd>Coverage for the extra cost of living elsewhere while your home is being repaired. Also called loss of use.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Amount of loss</dt><dd>The dollar value of a covered loss. It’s the only question an appraisal panel decides.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Appraisal clause</dt><dd>The policy provision that lets either party demand appraisal when they disagree on the amount of loss.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Appraisal award</dt><dd>The written decision signed by at least two of the three panel members setting the amount of loss.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Appraiser</dt><dd>The person each side names to value the loss in appraisal. Policies usually require appraisers to be competent and impartial.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt id="g-C">Coverage A</dt><dd>Dwelling coverage — the part of a homeowners policy that covers the house itself.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Coverage B</dt><dd>Other-structures coverage for detached garages, fences, sheds and similar buildings.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Coverage C</dt><dd>Personal property coverage for furniture, clothing, electronics and other belongings.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt id="g-D">Declarations page</dt><dd>The summary page of your policy listing coverages, limits, deductibles and endorsements.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Deductible</dt><dd>The amount you pay toward a covered loss before the policy pays.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Depreciation</dt><dd>The reduction in value of an item due to age, wear or obsolescence.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Derecho</dt><dd>A long-lived, widespread windstorm associated with a line of fast-moving thunderstorms.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Detach and reset</dt><dd>Removing an item, such as a gutter or light fixture, to perform a repair and then reinstalling it.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt id="g-E">Endorsement</dt><dd>A written change to a policy that adds, removes or modifies coverage.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Exclusion</dt><dd>A policy provision that removes coverage for certain causes or types of loss.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt id="g-F">FAIR Plan</dt><dd>A state-mandated insurer of last resort. In Illinois, the Illinois FAIR Plan Association.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt id="g-I">Ice and water shield</dt><dd>A self-adhering waterproof membrane installed at eaves, valleys and other vulnerable areas of a roof.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Ice dam</dt><dd>A ridge of ice at the edge of a roof that traps meltwater, which can then back up under shingles.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt id="g-L">Like kind and quality</dt><dd>The standard that replacement materials should be similar in type and quality to what was damaged.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Line item</dt><dd>A single entry in an estimate, such as a quantity of shingles or a square footage of drywall.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt id="g-M">Matching</dt><dd>Whether replacement materials reasonably match the undamaged materials next to them.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Mitigation</dt><dd>Steps taken to prevent further damage after a loss, such as tarping a roof or drying a basement.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt id="g-O">Ordinance or law coverage</dt><dd>Coverage for added costs of complying with building codes when repairing or rebuilding.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Overhead and profit (O&amp;P)</dt><dd>A general contractor’s charges for managing a job, typically added when several trades are involved.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt id="g-P">Percentage deductible</dt><dd>A deductible calculated as a percentage of the dwelling coverage limit rather than a flat dollar amount.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Proof of loss</dt><dd>A sworn statement of the amount claimed, which an insurer may request within a set time.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt id="g-R">Recoverable depreciation</dt><dd>Depreciation withheld from the first payment and paid once repairs or replacement are complete.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Replacement cost value (RCV)</dt><dd>The cost to repair or replace damaged property with like kind and quality, without depreciation.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Roofing square</dt><dd>A unit of roof area equal to 100 square feet.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt id="g-S">Scope of loss</dt><dd>The full list of damaged items and the repairs needed to restore them.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Section 155</dt><dd>A provision of the Illinois Insurance Code that lets courts award attorney fees and a limited penalty for vexatious and unreasonable claim delays or denials.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Sewer backup endorsement</dt><dd>An endorsement that adds coverage for water backing up through sewers or drains, usually with its own limit.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Sublimit</dt><dd>A lower limit within a policy that applies to a specific type of property or loss.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Suit limitation</dt><dd>A policy provision setting the time within which a lawsuit must be filed against the insurer.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Supplement</dt><dd>A request for additional payment for items missing or underpriced in an estimate.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt id="g-T">Test square</dt><dd>A 10-by-10-foot area of roof used to count and document hail impacts.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt id="g-U">Umpire</dt><dd>The neutral third member of an appraisal panel who decides items the appraisers can’t agree on.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt id="g-X">Xactimate</dt><dd>Estimating software widely used by insurers and contractors to price property repairs.</dd></div></dl></article>
<aside class="aside"><div class="box box-call"><h2 class="box-t">Talk it through first</h2><p>The first conversation is free. We’ll tell you plainly whether appraisal fits your claim.</p><a class="big" href="tel:+18445371427">+1 (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn full" href="/contact-an-appraiser/">Send claim details</a></div><div class="box"><h2 class="box-t">Resources</h2><ul class="box-list"><li><a href="/illinois-appraisal-process/">Illinois appraisal process</a></li><li><a href="/appraisal-questions/">Appraisal questions</a></li><li><a href="/insurance-claim-terms/" aria-current="page">Claim terms glossary</a></li><li><a href="/policyholder-articles/">Policyholder articles</a></li></ul></div></aside></div>
